  "response": "# Accessibility Design Review: BuggyRadioGroup\n\n**VERDICT: REVISE**\n\n**Overall Assessment**:\nThe `BuggyRadioGroup` component correctly utilizes native `<input type=\"radio\">` controls and wrapping `<label>` tags for individual choices, ensuring baseline keyboard arrow-key navigation and input naming. However, it fails fundamental accessibility design criteria by completely omitting semantic grouping (`<fieldset>`/`<legend>` or `role=\"radiogroup\"`), which leaves screen reader users with zero context for what the radio options belong to. Furthermore, hardcoding `name=\"options\"` creates a critical collision hazard across multiple instances on the same page, breaking form submission and cross-group keyboard boundaries.\n\n---\n\n### Pre-commitment Predictions\nBefore reviewing the component source, the following risks were anticipated for a custom radio group:\n1. **Missing Group Semantics**: Failure to use `<fieldset>` and `<legend>` (or ARIA equivalents), causing screen readers to announce options without announcing the group prompt/question. *(Confirmed)*\n2. **Hardcoded Name Collisions**: Non-unique `name` attributes causing mutual exclusion bugs across independent group instances. *(Confirmed)*\n3. **Broken Keyboard Model from div/span reimplementation**: Often custom radio groups reimplement selection with `<div>` and fail roving tabindex. *(Refuted: native `<input type=\"radio\">` was used, which preserves native arrow-key navigation).*\n4. **False claims in feature list**: Discrepancy between stated accessibility features and actual implementation. *(Confirmed: `htmlFor` was listed as \"properly used\" but is absent).*\n\n---\n\n## Major Findings\n\n### 1. Missing Semantic Grouping for Radio Controls\n- **Evidence**: [`BuggyRadioGroup.jsx:7-8`](file:///BuggyRadioGroup.jsx#L7-L8)\n  ```jsx\n  <div className=\"radio-group\">\n    <div className=\"group-label\">{label}</div>\n  ```\n- **Element**: `div.radio-group` and `div.group-label`\n- **Impacted Users**: Screen reader users (NVDA, JAWS, VoiceOver, TalkBack)\n- **Standard**: WCAG 2.2 Criterion 1.3.1 Info and Relationships (Level A); WAI-ARIA APG Radio Group Pattern\n- **Confidence**: HIGH\n- **Why this matters**:\n  Screen readers navigate radio buttons individually. When an AT user lands on an option (e.g., \"Standard Shipping\" or \"Yes\"), the screen reader announces only the option label, state, and position (e.g., *\"Radio button, checked, 1 of 3\"*). Because the enclosing label is an unlinked generic `<div>`, the user is never told what question or category they are answering. Native HTML provides `<fieldset>` and `<legend>` specifically to establish this relationship; when using native radio buttons, the browser automatically announces the legend upon entering the group.\n- **Fix**:\n  Replace the outer `<div>` with `<fieldset>` and the label container with `<legend>`. If styling constraints strictly forbid `<fieldset>`, use `role=\"radiogroup\"` on the container with an `id` on the label linked via `aria-labelledby`. Native HTML `<fieldset>` / `<legend>` is strongly preferred.\n\n```\n### A11y Evidence Finding\nfinding_id: radio-group-missing-semantic-grouping\nfingerprint: 8a4f21b7c0de9210\nsource: BuggyRadioGroup.jsx:7-8\nwcag_or_apg: WCAG 2.2 SC 1.3.1 (Info and Relationships) / WAI-ARIA APG Radio Group\nsection_508_fpc_context: WCAG 2.0 SC 1.3.1\nseverity: MAJOR\nperspective_alarms: screen_reader=HIGH, keyboard=LOW, low_vision=LOW, cognitive=MEDIUM\nevidence: Lines 7-8 use generic `<div className=\"radio-group\">` and `<div className=\"group-label\">` without `<fieldset>/<legend>` or `role=\"radiogroup\"`/`aria-labelledby`.\nreproduction_steps:\n  1. Render component with label=\"Shipping Method\" and options=[\"Standard\", \"Express\"].\n  2. Navigate to first radio button using VoiceOver/NVDA virtual cursor or Tab key.\n  3. Listen to announcement.\nexpected_behavior: Screen reader announces the group name (\"Shipping Method\") followed by the radio option and position.\nactual_behavior: Screen reader announces only the radio label (\"Standard, radio button, checked, 1 of 2\"). The group label is ignored.\ntrend: new\n```\n\n---\n\n### 2. Hardcoded Radio Group `name` Attribute (Instance Collision Hazard)\n- **Evidence**: [`BuggyRadioGroup.jsx:13`](file:///BuggyRadioGroup.jsx#L13)\n  ```jsx\n  <input\n    type=\"radio\"\n    name=\"options\"\n    value={option}\n  ...\n  ```\n- **Element**: `<input type=\"radio\" name=\"options\" />`\n- **Impacted Users**: Keyboard-only users, screen reader users, all form users\n- **Standard**: WCAG 2.2 Criterion 2.1.1 Keyboard (Level A), WCAG 2.2 Criterion 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value (Level A)\n- **Confidence**: HIGH\n- **Why this matters**:\n  In HTML, the `name` attribute defines the mutual-exclusion scope for radio buttons. Because `name=\"options\"` is hardcoded:\n  1. Rendering more than one `BuggyRadioGroup` on the same page/form causes all instances to share the same mutual-exclusion group. Selecting an option in Group B will deselect the selected option in Group A.\n  2. Keyboard arrow navigation links all radios sharing the same `name` in the document. A keyboard user attempting to cycle through options in Group A will jump directly into Group B upon reaching the edge, breaking logical focus and reading order.\n  3. Standard HTML form submission will submit only one `options` value across all groups.\n- **Fix**:\n  Accept a `name` prop (falling back to a unique generated identifier such as React 18's `useId()`):\n  ```jsx\n  const generatedId = useId();\n  const groupName = name || `radio-group-${generatedId}`;\n  ```\n\n```\n### A11y Evidence Finding\nfinding_id: radio-group-hardcoded-name-collision\nfingerprint: 3b92f7e109dca543\nsource: BuggyRadioGroup.jsx:13\nwcag_or_apg: WCAG 2.2 SC 2.1.1 (Keyboard) & SC 4.1.2 (Name, Role, Value)\nsection_508_fpc_context: WCAG 2.0 SC 2.1.1, 4.1.2\nseverity: MAJOR\nperspective_alarms: screen_reader=HIGH, keyboard=HIGH, low_vision=LOW, cognitive=HIGH\nevidence: Line 13 sets `name=\"options\"` as a static string literal on all rendered radio inputs.\nreproduction_steps:\n  1. Render two `<BuggyRadioGroup />` components within the same document.\n  2. Select an option in Group 1 with mouse or Space.\n  3. Use arrow keys to navigate options in Group 2.\nexpected_behavior: Each radio group operates independently with its own roving focus and selection.\nactual_behavior: Arrow keys cross into the second group; selection in one group deselects the other.\ntrend: new\n```\n\n---\n\n## Minor Findings\n\n1. **Incorrect Verification Claim Regarding `htmlFor`**:\n   - **Evidence**: Fixture metadata claims `\u2713 htmlFor properly used`.\n   - **Detail**: The component relies entirely on implicit labeling by wrapping the `<input>` inside the `<label>` tag. While wrapping is valid HTML, no `id` or `htmlFor` attributes exist. Implicit labeling can occasionally suffer from compatibility issues with certain legacy AT combinations (e.g., older VoiceOver versions and speech-input utilities like Dragon NaturallySpeaking). Providing explicit `id` and `htmlFor` attributes is more robust.\n2. **Uncontrolled Default Value on Empty `options`**:\n   - **Evidence**: [`BuggyRadioGroup.jsx:4`](file:///BuggyRadioGroup.jsx#L4) (`useState(options[0])`)\n   - **Detail**: If an empty array is passed, `selected` is initialized to `undefined`. If options load asynchronously, `selected` remains `undefined` even after items render, leaving none checked. While unselected radio groups are valid under WAI-ARIA APG, the component does not handle dynamic updates or prop-driven synchronization.\n\n---\n\n## What's Missing\n\n- **Native `<fieldset>` and `<legend>`**: Native grouping tag structure is missing entirely.\n- **Dynamic/Prop-driven `name` and `id` generation**: Missing `useId()` or unique prefix generator to guarantee isolated DOM scopes.\n- **Required / Disabled / Error State Communication**: Missing `aria-required`, `disabled`, or error associations (`aria-describedby`) for integration into validated forms (Anti-pattern Check: \"Broadcast vs. Association\").\n- **Visual Group Styling**: Lacks fieldset border or visual styling that communicates group boundaries to low vision and cognitive accessibility users.\n\n---\n\n## Multi-Perspective Notes\n\n- **Screen Reader User**:\n  - *Experience*: Severely degraded. Navigating via virtual cursor or Tab lands on an option without reading the prompt question. The user must manually navigate backwards in the DOM to read the preceding `<div>` to deduce what \"Option 1\" means.\n- **Keyboard-Only User**:\n  - *Experience*: Works correctly for a single isolated instance via native arrow keys and Tab. Completely broken if multiple groups exist on the same page due to the hardcoded `name` attribute.\n- **Low Vision User**:\n  - *Experience*: Depends on custom CSS. Because standard `<fieldset>` styling is not present, visual grouping depends on custom CSS classes (`.radio-group`, `.group-label`). Focus indicators default to native browser outlines, which is acceptable unless suppressed by CSS reset.\n- **Cognitive Accessibility**:\n  - *Experience*: High risk of error if multiple instances collide, creating unpredictable state mutations where choosing an answer in one section clears an earlier section.\n\n---\n\n## Perspective Alarm Levels\n\n| Perspective | Alarm Level | Trigger Signal |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Screen reader** | **HIGH** | Missing group name association (`<fieldset>/<legend>` or `radiogroup`/`aria-labelledby`). |\n| **Keyboard-only** | **HIGH** | Hardcoded `name=\"options\"` risks cross-widget arrow-key traps and deselect collisions. |\n| **Cognitive** | **MEDIUM** | Form answer context confusion and potential multi-instance state conflicts. |\n| **Low vision** | **LOW** | Standard text and native controls used; zoom reflow should follow standard block flow. |\n| **Vestibular & motion** | **LOW** | No motion, animations, or transitions present. |\n| **Auditory access** | **LOW** | No audio or video content. |\n| **Environmental contrast** | **MEDIUM** | Visual contrast of `.group-label` and custom radio styling must be verified against WCAG 1.4.3 (4.5:1). |\n\n---\n\n## Verdict Justification\n\n**Verdict: REVISE**\n\nAlthough the component correctly utilizes native `<input type=\"radio\">` controls (avoiding synthetic div-based roving tabindex anti-patterns), it misses the essential **semantic grouping layer** required by WCAG 1.3.1 and APG. Because radio buttons are meaningless without their category or prompt context, shipping this component as-is silently degrades the experience for screen reader users. Furthermore, the hardcoded `name` attribute represents an immediate regression hazard as soon as a second instance is added to any view.\n\n### Required Remediations Before Shipping:\n1. Replace `<div className=\"radio-group\">` and `<div className=\"group-label\">` with `<fieldset>` and `<legend>`.\n2. Generate unique `name` and `id` attributes using `React.useId()` or accept a `name` prop.\n3. Explicitly link `<label htmlFor={id}>` with `<input id={id}>`.\n\n### Recommended Code Remediation\n\n```jsx\nimport React, { useState, useId } from 'react';\n\nconst RadioGroup = ({ label, name, options = [], value, onChange }) => {\n  const generatedId = useId();\n  const groupName = name || `radio-group-${generatedId}`;\n  const [internalSelected, setInternalSelected] = useState(options[0]);\n\n  const selectedValue = value !== undefined ? value : internalSelected;\n\n  const handleChange = (val) => {\n    if (value === undefined) {\n      setInternalSelected(val);\n    }\n    onChange?.(val);\n  };\n\n  return (\n    <fieldset className=\"radio-group\">\n      <legend className=\"group-label\">{label}</legend>\n      {options.map((option, index) => {\n        const inputId = `${groupName}-opt-${index}`;\n        return (\n          <div key={option} className=\"radio-option\">\n            <input\n              type=\"radio\"\n              id={inputId}\n              name={groupName}\n              value={option}\n              checked={selectedValue === option}\n              onChange={(e) => handleChange(e.target.value)}\n            />\n            <label htmlFor={inputId} className=\"radio-label\">\n              {option}\n            </label>\n          </div>\n        );\n      })}\n    </fieldset>\n  );\n};\n\nexport default RadioGroup;\n```\n\n---\n\n## Open Questions (Unscored)\n1. **Form Validation & Error States**: Will this group participate in form validation? If so, error summaries or inline errors will need an `aria-describedby` association on the `<fieldset>` or individual inputs, and optional/required indicators should be placed in the `<legend>`.\n2. **Controlled Component Support**: Should this component operate as a fully controlled component (`value` and `onChange` props), or exclusively uncontrolled/self-contained?\n",
